WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.118
Judging asks you to evaluate the debate in front of you.

00:00:03.118 --> 00:00:05.679
Write down claims and responses on both sides.

00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:09.576
Avoid adding an argument a team never made simply because you know it.

00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:14.810
Your personal view of the resolution is different from your decision about who won
this round.

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:22.750
A useful ballot names the central clash, explains how each team handled it, and gives
a reason one comparison prevailed.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:26.457
Saying one team was more persuasive gives very little information.

00:00:26.457 --> 00:00:31.400
Instead describe the argument that survived and why it outweighed the remaining
offense.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.542
After the decision, give one specific strength and one next step.

00:00:36.542 --> 00:00:44.911
For example: your signposting made the rebuttal easy to follow; next time, spend
thirty seconds explaining why your impact is more likely.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:47.276
Keep feedback about observable choices.

00:00:47.276 --> 00:00:52.370
Speaker points follow the tournament rubric and are separate from personal approval.
